# For platforms without systemd. Install with `crontab -e` (per-user) or as a
# file under /etc/cron.d/ (system-wide; that form needs a user column between
# the schedule and the command, e.g. "0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/aub ...").
#
# Runs `aub coverage` once an hour. This is the alarm, and it is a separate
# entry from the sampling one on purpose: `aub sample` treats an authentication
# refusal as successfully recorded evidence and exits zero, so an account whose
# credential stopped working keeps the sampler quiet forever while its series
# goes dark. `aub coverage` exits with the ThresholdNotMet class (7) when an
# account falls below a configured floor.
#
# The redirect is the opposite of the sampling entry's and for the opposite
# reason. `aub coverage` prints its box on stdout on every run, breach or not,
# so leaving stdout alone would mail an unread box every hour and train the
# operator to filter the one mail that matters. The breach line goes to stderr:
#
#   aub: <account> attempt coverage 1.4% is below the 98% floor; ...
#
# cron mails whatever a job writes, so discarding stdout leaves exactly that
# line as the alarm. Do not redirect stderr as well.
0 * * * * /usr/local/bin/aub coverage >/dev/null
